Lwaxana:
  I've been around here for 12 years now.. I've had a pair banished for multi.
  I hate change in general, and change on things that are very close to me, in
  particular. So I find this rule disturbing, strange and hard to swallow. 
  BUT, at the same time, it paves the way for some very interesting and 
  rewarding experiences. I haven't used it much since it was put in place, 
  but I'm sure I will keep experimenting and eventually adapt and adopt it.

Auric:
  NOTE: This is not my final argument on multiplaying, it is just something I
  hacked together to give a quick notion of what my concerns are.  It doesn't
  go into detail about specific unbalances that might transpire; one could
  probably write a book on that!  If I repeat myself at all it's because I
  threw this together quickly.
  
  1.  Multiplaying was never taken into account when balancing the game.
  Introducing the ability to play with multiple characters from a
  single-keyboard introduces a new list of abuses that throw the game balance
  into disarray.
  
  2.  I've always considered the underpinning of the way this game is balanced
  to constantly deny a player from ever feeling truly powerful no matter how
  much effort he puts in, whilst giving him the occasional break that makes him
  powerful for a measured amount of time.  Thus the game always gives you
  something to aim for.  This has now been ruined.
  
  3.    All the hard work that went into considering the numbers inserted into
  various powers and equipment has been dismantled by multiplaying.
    
  4.  Unlike parties, your second-characters don't have to be motivated by
  self-interest.  The fact that before relying on other characters necessarily
  meant relying on other real humans was a limiting, balancing factor to
  whatever advantages another player could confer.  This balancing factor has
  ceased to exist; the only limitation now is your own ability to customize
  your client and automate whatever process you want to implement.
  
  5.  It defiles the team spirit of the game.
    
  6.  It disidentifies us with our characters and ruins immersion.
  Multiplaying is meta-game.  It takes us out of our character since we have to
  divide our mind between several characters at once.
  Worse, people cannot identify us.  They don't know who to talk to (which is
  almost worth a separate point, but I'll mention it here instead).  I've had
  many occasions where tells were missed because the person was focusing on a
  different character window.  They also might talk to different people not
  realising it is the same person.  The game is also socially hurt by removing
  any need to team up to solve a problem.
  
  7.  The power from simply having an unlimited-sized gang of characters under
  your control is--well, unlimited.
    
  8.  It kills PK.  Noone would multiplay and PK if they wanted to have fun,
  and noone would PK without multiplaying if they wanted to win.  Again, the
  limiting factor that more characters meant more human beings was a very real
  limiting factor, that no longer exists.  PK would (if there were more players
  doing it) degenerate into a contest of who can login the most characters!
  As well as all that it makes the softness of PK even softer than before;
  recuperation from a painful death (seems to only apply to knights?) is very
  easy.
